It's a very good weapon against many monsters, a perfectly decent one
(even without bonuses it's still a runesword, not a pick-axe or a
quarterstaff) against most others, it can be two-weaponed with a sabre
or even just blessed against demons, and the booby trap is something
that matters only when you have a pet (so usually irrelevant in the
later game), or around peacefuls (so unwield it inside temples, then).

> No other artifact suffers from as many drawbacks and delivers so little, 
> but only when wielded.

AFAICT, there's only one drawback: it attacks peacefuls. This drawback
is easy to avoid: unwield it around peacefuls. Around peacefuls _you
want to keep alive_, mind you: if you wield Stormy, chances are you are
chaotic, after all. 
It delivers quite a lot. Those lost hit-dice do help quite a bit, you
know, even without the +d5/+d2. OK, it doesn't do this against demons,
but hey, you can't have everything. Permanently draining levels is
particularly nice with the Wizard, for example.
It is so good, that I had an elven Ranger ascend with it as its main
weapon. Never mind that Longbow of Diana; that was nice for those few
occasions when Stormy didn't do well. Against Asmodeus I think I just
stood next to him and flung arrows at him point-blank. But even then:
the LoD's greatest advantage is multiple _poisoned_ arrows, and poison
doesn't work against demons any more than level-drain does. Everything
else? Just whack it with a well-enchanted Stormy, and it'll go away.

If you want a useless artifact, try Ogresmasher. It's a war hammer, so
against anything but ogres it's barely more useful than a dagger, and
who cares about extra damage to ogres, anyway?

> Now if only Sting did double damage against critters with posion 
> attacks,

Why? The Hobbit's Sting doesn't.

> The Longbow of Diana had an elvish nature, 

Why? Diana was a Greek goddess. There's nothing remotely elvish about
her.

IMO, there's no need to turn perfectly good artifacts into massive
killers. What we need is fewer useless artifacts, not more adamantium-
plated ones.
In fact, I wouldn't be against more _dis_-advantages for the stronger
weapons. And I don't mean that completely unimportant destruction of
potions of confusion of or wands of speed monster by Frosty and
Mjollnir; by the time you have to think about your ascension weapon, you
shouldn't need every single object in the dungeon any more. No, I mean
something halfway balancing. Like Excalibur causing everybody to find
you. If Grayswandir always acted as a cursed luckstone (just to mention
a random, probably unsuitable effect), someone who finds it might have
to think whether its attack was worth the downside.

